{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And a mist.</b> Smoke [vapor] in the vernacular, and this vav is superfluous [parasitic], and so there are many in Scripture. And so the interpretation: all the shrubs of the field — before they were added to what was created — a mist would go up from the earth by the force of the luminaries to water the ground and make it grow. And the statement כי לא המטיר intervenes to give the reason why the grasses and the trees continued to grow." "<b>יעלה.</b> An causative [hiph'il] form, like ויעל עלות במזבח, which is like העלה."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {0 "The vernacular gloss reads ברואינה, a medieval French word meaning 'vapor' or 'exhalation,' here labeled לע״ז (la'az, vernacular).", 1 "The Hebrew uses grammatical terminology: לשון מפעיל denotes the causative conjugation; the point is that יעלה functions transitively like העלה, not as a simple intransitive 'went up.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:02:23.821189271Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}